## Why DevOps Exists

### Waterfall Model

Waterfall model, where, you know, like when clients gives us some kind of requirement, let's say, for example, client asks us to create some application. So we gather the requirement first, asking them, like, what do you want and do you want, how do you want, all those. And then we will be assigning the task to the specific team people, right?

So what they will do, they will develop a code, and it will take around, like, one month or two months to develop the code according to the requirement. Then what happens is, like, after developing, they will be merging it together. But what happens, like sometimes one person would be using one version of Java, another person would be using another version of Java. So all these becomes a nuisance.

Then it will take some time to figure it out, and it all will be, like, getting that. So we will be, like, getting all of this done, right? We will be rectifying it, and then we will test the code.

So there will be a lot of bugs, our testing people will, like, clear it out, and then, like, that will take a bit of time. Then the actual testing will occur, like where the features are, if the features are working or not working, we will be testing it out.

For example, if a feature is supposed to share the file, whether it is sharing the file or it is just opening the link or it is just standing over there, we really doesn't know. So all this feature testing has to be done.

So even that is also done. So what we do, we will be, like deploying it, right? At last we will be, like, deploying it.

But what happens is, like, sometimes it will be, like, working in our system, and then, like when the client is taking up, it will be, like some kind of different kind of issues, there will be some kind of bug, and all those will be there.

So we will be, like, testing it all those, and we need to allocate some team to, like, monitor all those and then to observe whatever it is happening and all. So it was taking a lot of time.

### Agile

That's when Agile came up, right? So what was the difference between Waterfall and Agile?

Waterfall, they were doing it only, like, after one big step is completed, they take on another big step.

What Agile people decided is, like, okay, let's do it in a very simple way. What they will do is, like, they will do feature by feature.

For example, let's say, like, if user asks for a website, okay? First they will develop the front page, like what you called as interactive login page. They will do everything, and they make sure all the teams are together, collecting the requirements, understanding what is actually required, and once the feature is developed, they will test write it right away and they will deploy it right away.

It is called as, like, a sprint in their language. They will do it only, like, step by step.

So what happened is, like, all these were going on, right? So Agile, this is how Agile was following up.

But what happens is, like, even though all these were there, there were, like, a lot of gaps between certain things. That's when DevOps popped up, okay?

### DevOps

So what happens? Instantly, let's say, like, I write a code, I push it to the repository, like immediately it's tested and deployed, right? Like everything is instantly tested and deployed.

And what happens is, like, it creates a culture where, like all the people work together instead of blaming each other, they all start working together, where, like, let's say, like, I am writing something, my friend is also in the same project, deploying another feature, developing another feature, another feature.

So, like, once the code is written, it is, like immediately tested and it is, like, immediately deployed. And once it's deployed, the monitoring will also be going on, there will also be, like, observability something in case goes wrong.

What happens is, like, it will just reduce the gap between the development team and operation team by doing, like, you know, continuously it will merge the code, and also continuously it will deploy the code.

Okay. So that is what we call, like, even continuous integration and deployment, where whenever the code is getting pushed, and then it will be, like, continuously getting integrated and continuously getting deployed, like without waiting for some approval, or without doing this, and not without approval, but, like, yeah, continuously quick and quick, small and effective, without, like waiting for the whole process to be completed, right?

That's how the DevOps came on.

### Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)

So what Site Reliability Engineers, like, they are responsible for maintaining the overall reliability, as the name suggests.

Let's say, for example, some, if the application is running and around at 2 a.m. the server got crashed. So site reliability engineer will be the first one to get called up.

So what they will do? They will come over there and they will, like, certain analyze, like, what went wrong. They will check the alerts and then they will understand, like, where did it go wrong, why did it go wrong, and they will be able to certain understand and analyze, like, okay, this is what happened.

So, like, this is what has to be corrected, and they will be, like, correcting what went wrong over there. Okay? They will correct all of the things that went wrong.

These are the people who will be, like, point of contact for us. Okay?

Yeah. And they should always follow, like, SLA, SLI, and SLO.

So SLA is, like, for example, let's say I am the client. You will let me know, like, how much availability will be there, how much scalability will be there.

For example, if I have promised you, like 99.99 percentage of availability will be there, something goes wrong, for that 0.1 percentage I can mess up, but if something goes wrong, I need to pay you the penalty, right? So that's what the SLA is.

SLO is, like, service level objective, which means, like, how long— how quickly I will be available, very simple, how quickly I will be available.

For example, something goes wrong, the user raised an incident or anything, how quickly I can be available.

And then service level indicator is, like, how much, like, you know, error has happened, how much at the last we can, like, understand, like, okay, this is how much of request was raised, this is how much of error has occurred, and this is how much of latency has occurred.

So it's like a report. It can be, like, useful for us to understand and, like, correct the issue the next time, like, okay, this much latency has happened, so make sure to take the steps by analyzing the logs, metrics, and traces, and then we can make it better next time.

This much latency has happened, let's see what went wrong, whether the server was fucking it up, or, like, anything else was messing it up.

All these we can understand, and what error has contributed, that all we can understand.

Yeah, and then like SRE people, mostly they will be like on-call rotation because we never know when something will be happening, right? So they have to be on an on-call rotation, most companies.

And then there is something called error budget also, which will be like tagged to SREs.

Like, for example, what does error budget means? When I, when I, like, ensure a customer, like 99.99 percentage of availability will be there, that 0.01 percentage will be like the SRE's error budget.

### Platform Engineering

So now what is platform engineer?

So platform engineer is like, see, in DevOps we will be creating pipeline, we will be like pushing the code, pulling the code, testing it up, and then like automate something that has to be automated, and then we will make sure it is like continuously monitored.

Even if something is messed up, we use observability and then understand and resolve the issue, okay.

And SRE, as we said, like they are responsible for the errors, latency, and all those.

And platform engineer, you know, like they will be the one that is like, as the name suggests, they will be the one who providing the platform for making all of this happen.

Let's say if I am a DevOps engineer, I need to like, you know, login, I need to use a Docker, I need to maintain the Kubernetes, and all those.

I will be creating a file and then like just wait for the approval, and then like I will be able to use the Docker, and then like again get another approval and then like I will be using the Kubernetes.

So these are all like time-consuming.

So what happens is, right, platform engineer, they will create something.

They will create a platform and assign it to the site reliability engineer or to the DevOps people that everything is already pre-requested.

It's like providing the DevOps engineer with a template. Like everything is already there, where the DevOps people can just fill out the form and start using it without like waiting for all those approval and all those.

Okay. So that is like the thing.

So basically we will be like building the things and provide it to the people.

And also they will be having like a documentation, which is like very golden, because like you can understand, okay, this is how the thing works, and also if something error happens we can refer to the document and all those.

Here ends the topic of like what is DevOps and why DevOps is needed and what was the waterfall model and what was the agile model and what was the SRE do, what does the platform engineer do.


